	url = {https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-07765-5},
	doi = {10.1038/s41598-022-07765-5},
	abstract = {Abstract
            
              Native plants in extreme environments may harbor some unique microbial communities with particular functions to sustain their growth and tolerance to harsh conditions. The aim of this study was to investigate the bacterial communities profiles in some native plants and samples of the Moroccan phosphate mine ecosystem by assessing the percentages of taxonomic identification using six hypervariable regions of the 16S {rRNA}. The rhizosphere of the three wild plants in the Moroccan phosphate mine is characterized by interesting bacterial diversity including
